<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Sama (Kaabu)</h1><p class="st-meta">1500 CE – 1867 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">One of the three royal provinces of the Kaabu Empire, whose Nyancho nobles took turns supplying the empire's ruler.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Sama was a province of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaabu-empire">Mandinka Kaabu Empire</a> in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guinea-bissau">Guinea-Bissau</a>, which grew out of the westward expansion of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/mali-empire">Mali Empire</a> and became independent in the 16th century. Kaabu's history is known mainly from oral tradition and a few Portuguese accounts.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Sama, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/pachana-kaabu">Pachana</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/jimara-kaabu">Jimara</a> were the three royal provinces whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nyantio">Nyancho</a> warrior aristocracy, traced through the female line, provided the Mansaba, the supreme king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaabu-empire">Kaabu</a>, in rotation. Each was ruled by its own <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mansa">mansa</a>. The Nyancho lived largely from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slave-raiding">slave raiding</a> and trade with Portuguese ports.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Sama declined with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaabu-empire">Kaabu</a> in the 19th century under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fula">Fulani</a> attacks from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/imamate-of-futa-jallon">Imamate of Futa Jallon</a>. After the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kansala">Kansala</a> in 1867 the province was absorbed into Fula-ruled states and later <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/portuguese-guinea">Portuguese Guinea</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 1500 CE – 1867 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">West Africa</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:89.55%;width:7.30%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1500 CE – 1867 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Mandinka traditional religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Mandinka</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/guinea-bissau">Guinea-Bissau</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Sama, Sama Kaabu</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1684&amp;focus=sama-kaabu" aria-label="Sama (Kaabu) on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4287 1732 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Sama (Kaabu) lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4560 1941h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4607 1988h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M4607 1988h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Guinea-Bissau. <a href="/atlas?year=1684&amp;focus=sama-kaabu">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

